Android CI/CD with GitHub Actions - Complete Guide

This document is a step-by-step guide to setting up CI/CD for Android using GitHub Actions,
along with potential interview questions and answers for revision.

✅ Step-by-Step Setup

✅ Step 1: Create Android Project

Use Android Studio to create a new project
Language: Kotlin
Minimum SDK: API 21+

✅ Step 2: Initialize Git and Push to GitHub

git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git remote add origin https://github.com/your-username/your-repo-name.git
git push -u origin main

Note: Use a Personal Access Token (with repo and workflow scope) instead of a password

✅ Step 3: Add GitHub Actions Workflow

Create file:
.github/workflows/android-ci.yml

Basic CI Workflow:

name: Android CI

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main ]
  pull_request:

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4

    - name: Set up JDK 17
      uses: actions/setup-java@v4
      with:
        distribution: 'temurin'
        java-version: '17'

    - name: Grant Gradle Permission
      run: chmod +x gradlew

    - name: Build Debug APK
      run: ./gradlew assembleDebug

    - name: Run Unit Tests
      run: ./gradlew testDebugUnitTest

    - name: Run Lint
      run: ./gradlew lint

    - name: Upload Debug APK
      uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
      with:
        name: debug-apk-${{ github.run_number }}
        path: app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk

    - name: Set up Node.js
      uses: actions/setup-node@v4
      with:
        node-version: '20'

    - name: Install Firebase CLI
      run: npm install -g firebase-tools

    - name: Upload to Firebase
      run: |
        firebase appdistribution:distribute app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk \
          --app <your-app-id> \
          --token "${{ secrets.FIREBASE_TOKEN }}" \
          --groups "qa" \
          --debug

✅ Step 4: Firebase Setup

Create Firebase project and register Android app

Install Firebase CLI:
npm install -g firebase-tools
firebase login

Generate token:
firebase login:ci

Add the token as FIREBASE_TOKEN in GitHub repo secrets

Get Firebase App ID from console and update it in the workflow

✅ Optional: Validate Latest Build

Confirm by checking versionCode/versionName in Firebase Console

Use BuildConfig.VERSION_NAME in logs or splash screen

Use github.run_number to label builds


🎓 Interview Questions & Answers

Q1: What is CI/CD?

A: CI (Continuous Integration) is the practice of automatically building and testing code when
changes are pushed. CD (Continuous Deployment/Delivery) extends CI by automating the release process.

Q2: Why use GitHub Actions for Android CI/CD?

A: It's free, deeply integrated with GitHub, supports parallel jobs, and doesn't require
external CI tools.

Q3: What are common steps in an Android CI pipeline?

A: Checkout code, set up JDK, build APK, run tests, lint checks, upload artifact,
distribute via Firebase.

Q4: How do you authenticate Firebase CLI in GitHub Actions?

A: Use firebase login:ci to generate a token, then store it as a GitHub secret (FIREBASE_TOKEN).

Q5: What causes artifact already exists error?

A: Uploading an artifact with the same name in the same run.
Fix by appending ${{ github.run_number }}.

Q6: How to fix Firebase upload 404 error?

A: Make sure upload and distribute happen in the same job. The uploaded binary must exist and
the App ID must be correct.

Q7: What is the benefit of using run_number in artifact names?

A: Prevents conflicts in re-runs and helps identify unique builds.

